 ## Overview
-Ray traversal algorithms efficiently determine which nodes of a hierarchical spatial data structure (typically an octree) intersect a given ray, enabling the rendering of sparse voxel volumes by skipping empty space. This family solves the problem of $O(N)$ linear scanning in uniform grids by reducing complexity to $O(\log N)$ for sparse data. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) (which focuses on GPU-friendly linear memory layouts) or [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) (which focuses on connectivity) is the explicit management of ray parameters ($t$-values) and node states to minimize branching and memory accesses during the descent and ascent phases.
+Ray traversal algorithms determine the sequence of nodes in a spatial hierarchy (typically an octree) that a viewing ray intersects, enabling efficient volume rendering by skipping empty space. Unlike uniform grid ray marching, which samples every voxel, these methods use hierarchical descent to jump over large empty regions, significantly reducing computational cost for sparse datasets [S1, S6, S8, unofficial]. They differ from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) in their specific handling of implicit vs. explicit structures, and from [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) in their reliance on explicit backtracking or specific recursive descent logic.
 
